course: dictionary
lesson: 14684
title: Words that show a guess
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Presumably is an adverb. It tells you that something is likely, but nobody has checked it yet. It means probably, or as you would guess.
explain: «The lights are on and the car is outside, so Mum is presumably home.» Nobody has said so, but the clues make you expect it. Presumably shows a sensible guess, not a certain fact.
explain: Scientists use presumably too. «The seed has no light, so it presumably will not grow.» The word marks a guess. A scientist then tests the guess to find out if it is true.
fact: Presumably is an adverb, and it means as you would guess or expect; probably.
fact: Presumably shows that something is likely but not certain.
fact: «Presumably» is used in stories and everyday talk, and in science it marks a guess.
fact: If you find out the real answer, you do not need presumably any more.
exercise: «The lights are on, so Mum is presumably home.» What does presumably mean here? | Certainly, with no doubt at all | Yesterday | Never | *Probably
exercise: Jayden says «The bus has not come, so it is presumably stuck in traffic.» Which word shows that this is a guess, not a fact? | has | bus | *presumably | traffic
exercise: Ella asks, «Is presumably the same as definitely?» What is the answer? | *No, presumably means probably, which is less sure than definitely | No, presumably means very angry | Yes, they mean exactly the same | Yes, it means never
test: «The seed has no light, so it presumably will not grow.» Why does the scientist use presumably? | It means the seed is very big | It shows the seed is certainly dead | *It marks a guess that can be tested | It shows the light is too bright
test: Which sentence shows a likely guess, not a sure fact? | «The shop is open every day of the year.» | «The shop sells bread.» | *«Presumably the shop is open on Sunday.» | «The shop is closed, and I have the key.»
exam: Ruby says «He presumably missed the train.» Sam says «He missed the train.» Which sentence is a guess? | *Ruby's sentence | Sam's sentence | Neither has a verb | Both are sure facts
